# Assessment of Feature Brief 012, Incident Categorisation

## Verdict

The brief is a strong first draft and materially better than the previous brief on the same template (005, Surf Risk Rating). The Functional Requirements section captures the mechanics of the model accurately, nothing in it contradicts the pack, and the Type State and Tag requirements are close to dev-ready as written.

It is not yet ready to hand to the dev team. Three things stop it.

1. **Incident Outcome has no requirements.** The pack makes Outcome a required, controlled, Type-gated selection at close, and it is the mechanism that keeps a rescue searchable when it ends in a fatality. The brief mentions Outcome only in passing (configuration list, notifications, search). A developer working from the brief would not build it.
2. **The migration requirement is too narrow.** The brief says legacy types map to "an Incident Type or Incident Tag". Annex A maps them to Types, Tags, Outcomes, a Responding Agency, retirement (open as Not Final), removal, out-of-taxonomy, and in three cases a conditional split. The Migration Map itself is neither attached nor linked, despite the heading saying it should be.
3. **Five template sections are empty or still carry placeholder text** (Timeline, User Stories, Design/Mockups, Consultation, Future Improvements), and the Dependencies section lists one item where the pack names at least four.

Beyond those, the brief needs a copy-edit pass (nine grammar or incomplete-sentence defects, two of which are inside requirement statements), requirement numbering so the dev team can reference and test against it, and measurable performance targets in place of "promptly" and "without material delay".

Estimated effort to close: half a day of Flynn's time plus the attachments, most of which already exist in the pack.

