## Part A. Quality against the Feature Brief standard

The standard is the LIMSOC Feature Brief template, as evidenced by the section prompts carried in both this brief and brief 005. Each section is assessed for whether it answers its prompt at the depth a dev handoff needs.

### A1. Header and sign-off

| Item | Finding | Action |
|------|---------|--------|
| Brief number, version, author | Present. 012, v0.1, Flynn Young. | None |
| Date | "17/08", no year. Same convention as 005. | Use full date; this document will be referenced across seasons |
| Feature Category (1 to 5) | Not selected. Also unselected in 005, so this may be habitual. | Select. Recommend **2, High Impact, Recoverable**: the change touches every incident created and the SOP 4.3 notification matrix, but is configuration-driven and reversible. Category 1 is arguable if notifications are treated as safety-critical |
| Feature Owner | Flynn Young. The pack names Nicholas Wood as the model's author and feedback point. | Confirm the intended split: Flynn owns the feature delivery, Nick owns the model and its SME endorsement. State it in the Consultation section |
| Sign-off dates | Blank, expected at v0.1. | None |

### A2. Narrative sections

**Overview.** Answers the prompt. Names Type hierarchy, Type states, Tags, Agency Status, Incident Outcomes and Action Logs. Minor: "provide clearer, more consistent and structured approach" needs an article ("a clearer"). Consider one sentence on the single design principle from the pack (every value attached to an incident answers exactly one question, and no two mechanisms answer the same question). That principle is what stops developers and future admins adding fields that duplicate what Tags or Status already hold.

**Problem Statement.** Sound and consistent with Pack Part 1. Two gaps for a dev audience. First, none of the pack's concrete failure cases are carried (the rescue overwritten as a drowning and lost from Major Rescue searches; Marine Incident versus Vessel Incident recorded differently by shift; Resuscitation versus Recovered from Water with no rule between them). Developers turn concrete cases into test scenarios; abstract statements they cannot. Second, the seasonal cost (EMCaR manual interrogation of every data pull for SLSA, councils, LSV and clubs) is the business justification and is absent. Grammar: "reducing the reliability of operational data and requires significant manual interpretation" (subject mismatch; "and requiring").

**Impact.** Answers the prompt. "reduce the insights and accuracy of insights we are able to gain" repeats itself. The pack's Future Opportunities (AI-enabled features, guided incident creation, quality checks at point of entry, raising member expectations, national adoption) would strengthen this section and are absent.

**Proposed Solution.** Good. Correctly states Type limited to fundamental nature, Type states, guided questions, and Rule 1. Typo: "hierarchal" (hierarchical). Missing: the precedence rule for co-occurring drivers, and any statement that Incident Outcome is a required selection at close. Action Logs are named in the Overview but never defined anywhere in the brief.

### A3. Functional Requirements

Content coverage is assessed in Part B. This section assesses the requirements as a specification.

**Strengths.** Grouped logically by mechanism. The Type State group is precise and matches Annex C exactly. Tag scoping, inclusion/exclusion rules, refinement preserving history, and configuration-without-redevelopment are all well stated. The Search and Reporting and Incident Log groups add auditability and structured-storage requirements the pack only implies, which is a genuine improvement over the pack.

**Defects.**

| # | Location | Quoted text | Problem |
|---|----------|-------------|---------|
| 1 | Type States | "The system must allow incidents assigned to a Final Type, assuming all other requirements are complete." | Incomplete sentence. Missing "to be finalised (closed)" |
| 2 | Type States | "prevent users from selecting an Abstract Type when creating or updating an incident type" | Should read "creating or updating an incident". As written it describes admin editing of the taxonomy, not operator classification |
| 3 | Guided Questions | "Applying a controlled; or" | Missing word ("controlled Tag") |
| 4 | Incident Tags | "support inclusion of exclusion rules" | "inclusion or exclusion rules" |
| 5 | Incident Events | "retain its own Incident Type, Status, Outcome, Tags and and incident record" | Doubled "and"; "retains their own" |
| 6 | Legacy migration | Heading reads "Legacy Incident Type migration link to appendix with the required migration" | Author's note left in a heading; no appendix is attached or linked |
| 7 | Finalisation Validation | Second bullet: admin configures state of a Type | Duplicates Type States (first group) and Configuration Management. The first bullet duplicates "prevent an incident assigned to a Not Final type from being finalised" |
| 8 | Single Incident Type | Second bullet | Duplicates the second bullet of Incident Type Change Behaviour |
| 9 | Non-functional, Usability | "multiple devices, including tablet, desktop and pc" | Desktop and PC are the same thing. Intended list is probably mobile, tablet and desktop. Typo "heirarchy" under Scalability |

**Structural gaps.**

- **No requirement identifiers.** Brief 005 at least tiered requirements as Primary / Secondary / Tertiary. This brief has neither numbers nor priority. The dev team cannot reference, estimate, or write acceptance tests against unnumbered bullets, and the Consultation section cannot record which requirement a UAT finding relates to. Recommend FR-01 onward, grouped as they are now.
- **No prioritisation or phasing.** The pack itself separates the core model (tree, states, Tags, Status, Outcome, migration) from work that depends on other projects (full tag taxonomy with 1.8, guided question flows, Action Log, notifications). The brief presents all of it as one undifferentiated "must". A Must / Should / Later marking, or a Phase 1 / Phase 2 split, is needed before estimation.
- **No acceptance criteria.** Every requirement is a "must" statement without a test. The pack's worked scenarios (Part 2, Rules 2 and 3; Annex A row S07) are ready-made acceptance tests and are not used.
- **Mixed audiences.** Operator-facing, admin-portal, reporting and migration requirements are interleaved. A short tag on each group (Operator / Admin / Reporting / Migration) would let the dev team split the work.

### A4. Non-functional Requirements

Answers the prompt in shape but not in substance. "Without material delay", "promptly", "not materially degrade" and "same expected availability" are not testable. The dev team will either ignore them or ask. Recommend concrete figures agreed with the LIMSOC team, for example: Type picker and guided questions render within one second on the current LIMSOC baseline; automatic log entries for Type, Tag, Status and Outcome changes appear within five seconds; no regression in incident-creation time against the current median.

Missing NFR categories: role-based permissions (who can change a Type after opening, who can finalise, who can edit the taxonomy), audit retention, and accessibility. Security is not mentioned at all.

### A5. Timeline

Empty. The template asks for each stage. The pack ties the work to Season Priorities 2026-27 project 1.1, which implies a season-start target and a consultation window that has already begun. At minimum: SME endorsement date for the tree, configuration complete, UAT, training, cutover. If the dev team is being engaged now, this is the section they will read first.

### A6. Configuration Requirements

The list is complete and mirrors the Configuration Management group. The template also asks "how significant is the workload?" and that is not answered. It is significant, and the brief should say so: 72 legacy types to map (done in draft, Annex A), a full tag taxonomy that is not yet drafted (dependent on 1.8), guided question flows that do not exist yet, a Medical branch of 26 MPDS children, an Outcome set per Type, and a notification matrix to be rebuilt against SOP 4.3. Whoever owns each configuration item should be named.

### A7. Risks

The seven risks are reasonable. Two problems. First, the table formatting is broken: rows 2 to 7 carry list bullets inside every cell, so the table will not read cleanly in Word or when pasted into Jira. Second, the risk set misses the dependencies that actually threaten the feature:

- SOP 4.3 Incident Notifications is keyed on the legacy type codes (A01 to ZZZ) that the migration retires. The SOP must be revised and re-endorsed (COO endorsement, per its control page) in step with the cutover, or notifications silently stop matching. See Part C.
- Open model decisions (Welfare Check placement, Land/Water as children or Tags, whether SAR and Marine Incident are Not Final) gate the initial configuration. Configurability mitigates but does not remove this.
- The full tag taxonomy is not drafted and belongs to project 1.8. If 1.8 slips, Tags ship as a thin set.
- The Action Log (merging the first aid form and rescue log) is a separate piece of work the pack explicitly defers. If it is read as in scope for this brief, the estimate doubles.
- SLSA national reporting comparability. The pack states "National is expected to follow LSV's lead". Until it does, season returns to SLSA need a mapping from the new model back to whatever national requires.
- Cutover of open incidents mid-season (neither document covers this; see Part C).

### A8. User Stories, Design / Mockups

User Stories still carries the template placeholder text ("As a… I want to… So that…"). Either populate or delete. Populating is recommended: the pack's worked scenarios convert directly.

- As a Comms operator, I open an incident as Medical while the presenting problem is unknown, and the system prevents me closing it until I refine it to a Final child.
- As a Comms operator, when a rescue ends in a fatality I record Outcome: Fatality and the incident remains a Rescue.
- As a Comms operator, when AV asks us to carry their patient off the beach I open External Agency > Tasking; when we are managing the patient and call AV, I open Medical with AV as a responding service.
- As a Duty Officer, when a Rescue Equipment Activation turns into a real rescue, the incident stays typed as the activation and I progress Agency Status, Tags and Outcome.
- As the EMCaR reporting lead, I filter closed incidents by Outcome: Fatality across all Types without relying on the Type having been changed.

Design / Mockups has nothing attached. The essential attachments already exist in the pack: the full type tree with states (Part 2, The types, plus the Medical branch), the Type State behaviour table, the supplementary fields table, and the Migration Map (Annex A). Without the tree, the dev team has no picture of the taxonomy it is building the hierarchy for.

### A9. Dependencies / Constraints

One item: "Large notification changes." The pack names Simplified Incident Creation as a prerequisite and Incident Labels (1.8) as one effort with this. SOP 4.3 and SOP 4.4 (Activity Logs) are affected. The adaptive first aid form is to become a section of the Action Log. None of these are listed.

### A10. Consultation

Empty table. The pack names the Incident Categorisation and Flow SME group, the EMCaR reporting team, LSV Comms operators, State Duty Officers, Area Lifesaving Ops Officers, and the State Emergency Management Officer (who manages the automated paging list per SOP 4.3). UAT participants and the endorsement path for the taxonomy (SME group, then whom) should be named.

### A11. Future Improvements

Empty. Pack Part 2, Future Opportunities, and Part 4 open items supply this directly: smarter guided questions over time, AI-enabled guided creation and point-of-entry quality checks, automated summaries, the summarised incident view for command, and national adoption.

### A12. Register and style

Register is appropriate for an internal LSV document. Australian English is used consistently. No em dashes. Sentences are occasionally long but clear. The copy-edit items in A3 are the only style corrections needed.

