## Part C. Gaps in both documents that the dev team will raise

These are not in the brief and are not in the pack either. They surfaced from reading SOP 4.3 alongside the model.

1. **SOP 4.3 is keyed on legacy type codes.** The notification matrix lists who is notified by Incident Type number (A01, D01, E02, S01 and so on). The model retires those codes and collapses many of them (six shark sighting types become one Type with two Tag dimensions). Neither document says how the matrix carries over. The dev impact is a rules engine keyed on Type, Tag, Status and Outcome combinations rather than a single type code; the governance impact is a revised SOP 4.3 re-endorsed by the COO before cutover. Recommend the brief carries a requirement that notification rules can be expressed on any combination of Type (including parent nodes), Tag, Agency Status and Outcome, and a dependency on SOP 4.3 revision.
2. **SOP 4.3 has cross-cutting conditions that are not types.** The matrix includes "Any member injury" (DZZ), "Any incident utilising state resources", "Any incident involving more than one club or service", "Any incident with potential media interest", and "Notified if protracted" on missing person incidents. In the model these are Tags (Member Injury and Multi-agency exist; State Resources, Media Interest and Multi-club do not) or a duration condition (protracted). The tag taxonomy work with 1.8 should include them, and the notification engine needs a time-based trigger or a Protracted Tag.
3. **Stable Type identifiers.** Current types carry codes used by SOP 4.3, the paging list managed by the State Emergency Management Officer, and SLSA reporting. The model defines names only. Each node needs a stable code or ID that survives renaming and re-parenting, so that reports, paging and SOPs reference something durable.
4. **Cutover of open incidents.** Neither document says what happens to incidents open at cutover, or whether the change lands at season start as a single switch or dual-runs. Recommend: cutover between seasons, open incidents at cutover migrate by the same rules as historical records, and the legacy picker is removed rather than dual-run.
5. **Permissions.** Who may change a Type after opening (Rule 1 makes this a correction, which some organisations restrict to supervisors), who may finalise, and who may edit the taxonomy, question flows and notification rules. Not addressed in either document.
6. **Outcome and closure of Information Only incidents.** Agency Status Information Only is an advisory posture. Whether such incidents require an Outcome at close, and which Outcome set they surface, is not stated.
7. **Reporting continuity across the cutover.** Season statistics will span both models in the transition year. The brief's "historical types remain identifiable" covers the storage; a requirement that reports can aggregate legacy and new records under the new tree (via the migration map) covers the use.
8. **Taxonomy versioning.** The brief requires the tree to be editable without redevelopment, but says nothing about what happens to closed incidents when a node is renamed, re-parented, retired or has its state changed. Closed incidents must keep the classification they closed with, and reports must be able to read the tree as it stood at any date. Same applies to Tag, Status and Outcome value sets.
9. **Mutually exclusive Tag groups.** Several of the pack's modifier Tags are exclusive pairs (Confirmed / Reported; Injury / No Injury; a single Confirmation Source). The brief's Tag model allows any number of Tags with inclusion and exclusion by Type only. The configuration model needs exclusive groups, and possibly a maximum-one-per-group rule, or these become free-for-all.
10. **Guided-question answers as data.** The pack says answers populate the existing primitives (Type, Tag, responding service, a few structured fields). Whether the answer itself is stored (for audit and for re-running a changed question flow), whether questions can be skipped or answered Unknown, and whether an operator can go back up the tree are not stated. The dev team will need a decision on each.
11. **Migration sign-off and rollback.** Annex A is a draft with flagged rows. The brief needs to name who signs off the final mapping, how unmappable or conditional rows are handled (see B7), and whether the migration is reversible if the mapping is found wrong after cutover.

